#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_jacobi_nc

#% text
# `inverse_jacobi_nc(v, m)` gives the `u` with `jacobi_nc(u, m) = v`, the inverse of the Jacobi elliptic function `nc`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is the principal branch of DLMF 22.15, real for a real `v` in the range of the function on the real line and `0 <= m < 1`.
#% item
# For a real `m > 1` the value is `inverse_jacobi_nd(v, 1/m)/sqrt(m)` (the reciprocal-modulus transformation, DLMF 22.17), real for a real `v` in the range of the function on the real line.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values; exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_nc(2.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 1.1424290580457774

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_nc(1.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_nc(5.0, 0.3)
#% output
#= 1.473906772631354

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `jacobi_nc` undoes it:

#% cell
jacobi_nc(inverse_jacobi_nc(2.0, 0.4), 0.4)
#% output
#= 2.0000000000000004

#% text
# With `m = 0` the Jacobi functions are trigonometric functions, so the inverse is a trigonometric inverse:

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_nc(2.0, 0)
#% output
#= 1.0471975511965979

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `jacobi_nc`, `inverse_jacobi_sn`, `jacobi_amplitude`, `elliptic_f`
